Let $X$ be a closed connected oriented smooth four-manifold, and let $\omega=(\omega_1,\omega_2,\omega_3)$ be a smooth hypersymplectic structure normalized by $$\label{eq:intro-normalization}
 \int_X\omega_i\wedge\omega_j=\delta_{ij}.$$ There is a smooth family of hypersymplectic structures $\omega(t)$, $0\le t\le1$, such that $$\omega(0)=\omega,\qquad [\omega_i(t)]=[\omega_i]
 \quad (i=1,2,3),$$ and a positive volume form $\mu_1$ with $$\label{eq:intro-endpoint}
 \omega_i(1)\wedge\omega_j(1)=2\delta_{ij}\mu_1.$$ The endpoint forms are parallel and self-dual for a hyperkähler metric.

There is a classical pseudodifferential operator $D:C^\infty(X,E)\longrightarrow\Omega^2(X)$ of order zero with $$\label{eq:current-lift-identities}
 \mathrm dD=0,\qquad RD=\mathop{\mathrm{Id}}.$$ It maps distributions to distributions. For the separator in <span class="upright">(i)</span>, define $$\label{eq:current-correction}
 Q(\xi)=-N(DR\xi),\qquad T=N+Q.$$ Then $Q$ is anti-invariant and self-dual, $Q,T\in H^{-3}$, and $T$ annihilates every closed smooth two-form. In particular $T$ is a closed current and $[*T]=0$.*

*For a smooth family of $(J,g)$ with constant dimension of the space of closed anti-invariant forms, these lifts can be chosen smoothly in the parameter as operators between the corresponding smooth bundles.

Choose a fixed radius $r_0$ smaller than the injectivity radius, and let $\tau_{z\to y}$ be parallel transport along the minimizing geodesic when $\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)<r_0$. The kernel $K_s$ of $S_s^2$ can be written $$\label{eq:current-kernel-split}
 K_s(y,z)=k_s(y,z)\tau_{z\to y}+E_s(y,z),$$ where $k_s$ is nonnegative and supported where $\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)<r_0$. For each integer $k\ge0$ there are constants such that $$\begin{aligned}
 k_s(y,z)&\ge cs^{-4}&&\text{if }\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)<s,
                         \label{eq:current-kernel-lower}\\
 k_s(y,z)&\le C_ks^{-4}(1+\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)/s)^{-k},
                         \label{eq:current-kernel-upper}\\
 |E_s(y,z)|&\le C_ks^{-2}(1+\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)/s)^{-k}.
                         \label{eq:current-kernel-error}
\end{aligned}$$ The small upper bound on $s_0$ is fixed independently of $y,z$.

For any $\lambda_1,\lambda_2\le\lambda$ and their currents, $$\begin{aligned}
 \langle S_sN_1,*S_sN_2\rangle_2
 &\ge cs^{-4}
       \iint_{\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)<s}|\ell-\tau_{z\to y}m|^2
             \,\mathrm d\lambda_1(y,\ell)\mathrm d\lambda_2(z,m)
       \notag\\[-2pt]
 &\hspace{8mm}-C\int_X B_{s,\mu_1}(z)\,\mathrm d\mu_2(z),
                                  \label{eq:current-positive-pairing}\\
 \|S_sN_i\|_2&\le C/s.            \label{eq:current-submeasure-ltwo}
\end{aligned}$$ One can replace $B_{s,\mu_1}$ in the lower bound by $B_s$.

If $\Pi$ is a smooth orthogonal bundle projection and $\Pi W=0$ for a distributional form $W$ with $S_sW\in L^2$, then $$\label{eq:current-projection-leakage}
 \|\Pi S_sW\|_2\le Cs\|S_sW\|_2.$$ Consequently, for every positive submeasure current $N'\le_\lambda N$ and every anti-invariant distribution $Z$ with $S_sZ\in L^2$, $$\label{eq:current-mixed-bound}
 |\langle S_sN',S_sZ\rangle_2|\le C\|S_sZ\|_2.$$ Here $N'\le_\lambda N$ means that $N'$ is represented by some $\lambda'\le\lambda$.

In setup <span class="upright">(A)</span> or <span class="upright">(B)</span>, the correction satisfies $Q\in L^2(X,E)$ and $$\label{eq:current-angular-energy}
 \iint_{\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)<s}|\ell-\tau_{z\to y}m|^2
           \,\mathrm d\lambda(y,\ell)\mathrm d\lambda(z,m)\le Cs^4.$$ The estimates in [lem:current-positive-pairing](#lem:current-positive-pairing), [lem:current-commutator](#lem:current-commutator) hold for all the indicated submeasures, and, for every such fixed submeasure current $N'$, $$\label{eq:current-mixed-vanishing}
 \lim_{s\downarrow0}\langle S_sN',S_sQ\rangle_2=0.$$ In fact the convergence in [eq:current-mixed-vanishing](#eq:current-mixed-vanishing) is uniform over $\lambda'\le\lambda$.

With the plane projection taken in $T_yX$, one has $$\label{eq:density-transverse}
 \mathcal T_s:=
 \int d\lambda(y,\ell)
 \int_{B_s(y)}
 \left|\operatorname{pr}_{\ell^\perp}
                \exp_y^{-1}(z)/s\right|^2\,d\mu(z)
 =o(s^2).$$

Set $$B_s(z)=s^{-2}\int_X(1+\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,z)/s)^{-6}\,d\mu(y).$$ These functions are uniformly bounded, and $B_s(z)\to0$ whenever $\vartheta(z)=0$. In particular, $$\label{eq:density-diffuse-error}
 \int_X B_s(z)\,d\mu^d(z)\longrightarrow0,\qquad
 \mu^d=\mathbf1_{\{\vartheta=0\}}\mu.$$

Suppose $E^2\ge-2$, and put $q=(E^2+2)/2$. For the product manifold family over $S^2$, with the fixed $\mathfrak s_E$ just specified, the difference between two chamber evaluations of $U_p^q$ is $$\label{eq:families-wall-formula}
  \pm\deg(\text{difference of wall sections})
       \int_{\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}^0(X)}
          s_{b_1/2}(\operatorname{Ind}\not D_E).$$ Here $s$ denotes the inverse total Chern class, and the Dirac family over $\mathop{\mathrm{Pic}}^0(X)$ uses the universal flat twisting line normalized at $p$. The constant family evaluation is zero. For the large-parameter family [eq:families-taubes-equations](#eq:families-taubes-equations) associated with $\eta_v$, the degree difference from a constant family has absolute value one.
